## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep knowledge of bit manipulation techniques for combinatorial enumeration without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Next higher number with same popcount (snoob algorithm)
- Handling 64-bit overflow with masking
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts only
- Edge cases are constant-time checks

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextPermutation64(v) {
  const MASK = (1n << 64n) - 1n;
  if (v === 0n || v === MASK) return 0n;
  const c = v & -v;
  const ripple = v + c;
  const ones = v ^ ripple;
  const next = ripple | ((ones >> 2n) / c);
  return next & MASK;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced 128-bit bit-twiddling, boundary shifts, and correctness under rotation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Rotation across a 64-bit boundary
- Handling n in [0,127] with minimal branching in production

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  n &= 127; // modulo 128
  if (n < 64){
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned k = n - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
    uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you make it fully branchless with masks?
- How would you adapt for different word sizes or endianness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit rotation across word boundary without per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; cross-word mixing; edge k<64 vs k>=64

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi, lo; } uint128_t;
static inline uint128_t rotl128(uint128_t x, unsigned k){
  k %= 128; uint128_t r;
  if (k < 64){
    r.hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    r.lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
  } else { unsigned kk = k - 64;
    r.hi = (x.lo << kk) | (x.hi >> (64 - kk));
    r.lo = (x.hi << kk) | (x.lo >> (64 - kk));
  }
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test boundary cases k=0, 63, 64, 127?
- Could you adapt to 256-bit using 4x uint64_t without loops?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes low-level bit-tracking across a 128-bit value, edge cases at zero, and portable handling when single-word intrinsics are unavailable. It mirrors real-world tasks where bitmaps or flags span multiple words.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit split into two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count logic with early non-zero word optimizations
- Portable fallback for environments lacking 64-bit ctzn intrinsics
- Edge case for zero input

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  if (x.lo) return __builtin_ctzll(x.lo);
  if (x.hi) return 64 + __builtin_ctzll(x.hi);
  return 128;
}
```

