- How would you extend to arbitrary bit widths or detect overflow across the 128-bit boundary?
- How would you unit-test edge cases: all zeros, all ones, alternating patterns, and patterns crossing the boundary?

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to extend bit manipulation techniques to multi-register data structures while maintaining branchless performance.

## Key Concepts
- Two's complement negation for 64-bit words
- Isolating least significant set bit with x & -x
- Branchless masking using arithmetic on boolean predicates

## Code Example
```javascript
void isolateLeastBit128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_lsb = lo & -lo;
  uint64_t hi_lsb = hi & -hi;
  uint64_t nonzero_lo = -(lo != 0);
  uint64_t zero_lo = -(lo == 0);
  *out_lo = lo_lsb & nonzero_lo;
  *out_hi = hi_lsb & zero_lo;
}
```

## Example
Input: hi=0x8000, lo=0x0C00 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0400
Input: hi=0x0000, lo=0x0000 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0000

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bitwise folding to compute parity without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parity via XOR folding
- Bitwise shifts to mix bits
- No branches or loops in parity path

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t parity32(uint32_t n) {
  uint32_t p = n;
  p ^= p >> 16;
  p ^= p >> 8;
  p ^= p >> 4;
  p ^= p >> 2;
  p ^= p >> 1;
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt parity32 for 64-bit inputs efficiently?
- What are the trade-offs compared to using a popcount-based method?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit interleaving without loops, a common low-level optimization in graphics/encoding.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (Morton order)
- Mask-and-shift interleaving
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudocode outline (no branches)
uint128 interleaveBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  uint64_t sLo = spreadBy2(lo); // using masks 0x5555.., 0x3333.., etc.
  uint64_t sHi = spreadBy2(hi);
  r.lo = sLo;
  r.hi = sHi << 1;
  return r;
}
```
